## 4a. Prose is written through `lesson-scribe`, not by the forger inline

Every B1 paragraph of a CT lesson from U2 forward is drafted by the studio's
[`lesson-scribe`](~/src/.cursor/skills/lesson-scribe/SKILL.md) skill —
`ct-unit-forge` supplies structure, evidence, and cite rules; `lesson-scribe`
orchestrates `thessia-scholar-v3` (local Ollama, per studio local-first
covenant), records every prompt/response/amendment in
`~/src/deviac/fine-tuning/thessia-tuning-memory/`, and returns integrated
prose whose voice is checkable against previous units.

The forger MUST hand `lesson-scribe` three grounded inputs per Masterclass
idea (never a bare topic — the same discipline `tribune-forger` §4 already
enforces):

| Input | Source | What it contains |
| ----- | ------ | ---------------- |
| **(a) Thesis** | this unit's row in the course flow | one sentence naming which stream of the course this idea advances |
| **(b) Grounded path** | `profield-creativity-techniques` + `unit-enrichment/U*/PROFESSOR.md` + `grounding/pass1.edited.md` | the reasonable path leading to this idea — which authors' consolidated positions, which trained abilities, which pass-1 findings |
| **(c) Athanor findings** | `athanor search --project-slug profield-creativity-techniques` (+ cross-project neural search when a synergy is suspected) | synergies/sinastrias with related matches; explicit **gaps** kept for later, not smoothed away |

Style contract (borrowed from `tribune-forger` §2c — Wilson axis, selective
techniques): **be concrete, be precise, ask Thessia to be the author.** One
main idea per paragraph (surprise). Secondary sentence: explanation + example.
Tertiary sentence: explanation + open question. Closing sentence: discussion,
counter-critics, paradoxical incapacity of closing — Thessia knows *antilism*;
tell it to use, never to mention. Do not invent evidence; only cite what §6
of `curriculum-forger` licenses.

**Tribune readability pass:** after drafting, ask Thessia to return a chunked
rewrite with one claim per paragraph, short concrete verbs, one example, and
one open question. Keep the scholar's cadence and conceptual tension, but cut
process labels, source-workflow language, nested qualifications, and sentences
longer than two clauses where meaning is not gained. Review chunks side by side;
accept only changes that preserve the grounded claim and the author's voice.
Store rejected and accepted chunks in the tuning record so the next round can
learn from the comparison.

**Student-surface firewall:** editor addresses, forge instructions, harness
names, retrieval metadata, IDs, coats, node paths, and unresolved procurement
notes must not appear in the student lesson body or deck. If a limit must be
recorded, put it in the final `Editorial note` only; provenance remains in a
switch-gated `curriculum-internal` comment. The only student-facing exception
is a demanded TTOD index: its quote `id`, number, form, and controlled fields
must be explicit in the data contract, but must not be rendered as editorial
workflow text.

Length target per unit: **tribune-length lesson** (~1500–2200 words body,
plus TOC + References) matching U1's shape. TOC via `{:toc}` after the H2.

## 4a-bis. Every lesson ships a `## Conclusion` (pedagogical honesty section)

Every forged lesson closes its body — **immediately before** `## References` —
with a `## Conclusion` section that names the pedagogical bet: **identified
gaps within related thematic fields can naturally emerge from a lesson like
this, sparking learner motivation to explore and deepen their knowledge**.
This is not a summary of what the lesson said; it is a short paragraph that
tells the student what the lesson **did not close**, and why that is content,
not embarrassment.

Written in the operator's voice — teenager-legible, concrete, refused-close.
Drafted through `lesson-scribe` under the same six-axis discipline as any
Masterclass paragraph (its tuning-memory record slug is
`<course>-u<N>-conclusion`).

Boilerplate frame (fill per lesson — do not paste literally):

```markdown
## Conclusion

<One paragraph, four sentences, tribune shape. Sentence 1 surprises with a
concrete observation about what the unit *did not* settle. Sentence 2
names one open thematic field the student could push into — with a real
pointer (a book, a studio, a career path from the Directory), not a
gesture. Sentence 3 asks the student what they will do with the gap.
Sentence 4 closes without closing — the gap is the invitation.>
```

## 4a-ter. Editorial note after References (Teaching Innovation Practice)

Do **not** place “What the sources support…” (or any epistemic-limit editorial)
between Masterclass paragraphs and Lab. After `## References`, before
`## AI-assisted authorship`, ship:

```markdown
## Editorial note. Work in progress. Teaching Innovation Practice
{: .lesson-editorial-note }

<What page-verified sources support · what they do not · open cites.
No new teaching content.>
```

This is the *Práctica de Innovación docente* transparency surface. Only the
AI-authorship declaration may follow.

## 4c. Lesson page chrome + agentic Cover + Practice anchor

Visible student order after TOC:

1. Sticky chrome (layout): breadcrumb + lang/deck **button bar** (`deck_url`).
2. Header.
3. Opening block in `<div class="lesson-opener" markdown="1">`: TTOD quote +
   `{% include lesson-semantic-graphic.html %}`.
4. Learning objectives → Analysis → Masterclass → Lab → Workshop → Conclusion →
   References → Editorial note → AI declaration.

**Cover is commented / agentic — never a visible H2.** Store CONTENIDOS, one-line
stake, class rhythm, and grade feeds in `{% comment %} cover-agentic: … {% endcomment %}`.

| Field | Rule |
| ----- | ---- |
| **Master idea** | One claim students can repeat |
| **Practice anchor** | Durable operations/constructs to rehearse — **not** a tool UI |
| **Frontier signal** | Optional named gap |

## 4b. Every lesson ships an AI-usage declaration + internal UUID/date

Every forged lesson closes with an `## AI-assisted authorship` section that:

1. States that **Rubén Vega Balbás, PhD** (`ruvebal@crea-comm.net`) **wrote**
   the lesson — human authorship first, not a disclaimer afterthought.
2. Names the crea-comm.net studio stack in plain roles (publication firewall —
   no Ahmes/Athanor/DevIAC product names on the student surface): local
   *agentic harness*, *MCP* retrieval, *RAG* over the curriculum vault, and a
   *scholar-voice model* fine-tuned on the author's own writing; plus
   page-verified extraction and a citation-fidelity gate.
3. States how many vault sources were consulted for this forge
